      <!-- Section 2 -->
      <section id="alcohol-concentration-and-solubility" class="section">
        <h2>Alcohol Concentration & Solubility</h2>
        <p>Denatured ethanol is the primary solvent in most aerosol fragrance formulations because it fully solubilizes fragrance oils while evaporating cleanly from skin without leaving residue. The alcohol concentration in the formula directly governs how much fragrance oil can remain in a single, clear phase — higher alcohol content supports higher oil loads, which is why fine fragrance aerosols lean toward higher-proof bases than body mists.</p>
        <p>Alcohol concentration also controls evaporation kinetics, which shapes how a fragrance is perceived over time. A higher-alcohol formulation flashes off quickly, releasing top notes in a sharp initial burst before settling into heart and base notes. Body mists, formulated with more water relative to alcohol, evaporate more slowly and deliver a cooler, softer sensory profile on application — a deliberate trade-off since body mists are designed for frequent reapplication rather than long-wear projection.</p>
        <div class="table-container" style="overflow-x: auto; margin: 24px 0;">
          <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; text-align: left; font-size: 0.95rem;">
            <thead>
              <tr style="background: var(--primary); color: white;">
                <th style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Formulation Tier</th>
                <th style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Relative Fragrance Oil Load</th>
                <th style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Relative Alcohol Base</th>
                <th style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Sensory Profile</th>
              </tr>
