                <td style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">High (eau de toilette / parfum strength)</td>
                <td style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Higher-proof denatured alcohol</td>
                <td style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Strong initial burst, targeted, longer wear</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Chemical property data for common fragrance solvents and denatured alcohol grades, including solubility parameters and vapor pressure, can be referenced through the National Institutes of Health's <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubChem Database</a>, which formulators use to evaluate solvent compatibility during development.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <section id="propellant-selection-and-atomization" class="section">
        <h2>Propellant Selection & Atomization</h2>
        <p>The propellant system in an aerosol fragrance product must generate sufficient vapor pressure to atomize the alcohol-water-fragrance mixture into a fine, even mist without disrupting the scent profile. Most body mists and fine fragrance aerosols use liquefied petroleum gas blends of butane, isobutane, and propane, or dimethyl ether (DME), chosen for their compatibility with alcohol-based systems and cost efficiency at scale.</p>
        <p>Premium fine fragrance aerosols occasionally use compressed gas propellants, such as nitrogen, instead of liquefied hydrocarbon gases. Compressed gas propellants carry no inherent odor and do not condense back into the product, which avoids introducing any hydrocarbon note into a carefully balanced fragrance composition. This choice requires higher fill pressures and valve components engineered for constant-pressure delivery, since compressed gas systems lose pressure progressively as the can empties rather than maintaining the near-constant pressure of a liquefied gas system.</p>
        <p>For a deeper comparison of how compressed and liquefied propellant systems behave under use, see our guide on <a href="/compressed-vs-liquefied-gas-aerosols-pressure-profiles/" target="_top">compressed gas vs liquefied gas aerosols</a>. The full range of propellant chemistries available to formulators, including their vapor pressure and compatibility characteristics, is detailed in our <a href="/aerosol-propellants-explained-lpg-vs-dme-vs-hfc-vs-n2-vs-co2/" target="_top">aerosol propellants explained</a> resource.</p>
        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary); background: #f0f9ff; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--primary); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Key Insight</strong>
          Propellant choice in fragrance aerosols is not purely a pressure-delivery decision — it is a sensory decision, since any residual hydrocarbon odor from the propellant can distort the top notes of a carefully balanced fragrance composition.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4 -->
      <section id="projection-and-droplet-size-engineering" class="section">
        <h2>Projection & Droplet Size Engineering</h2>
        <p>Droplet size distribution, controlled primarily through actuator orifice geometry and valve design, determines how a fragrance aerosol behaves once it leaves the can. Body mists are typically engineered for smaller, more dispersed droplets that create a diffuse cloud suited to light, all-over application across the arms, torso, and legs. This fine atomization spreads the lower fragrance oil concentration evenly, avoiding localized over-saturation.</p>
        <p>Fine fragrance aerosols are often engineered with a slightly coarser, more directed spray pattern so a greater proportion of product lands precisely on the target application zone, such as the neck or wrists. This improves fragrance longevity and perceived projection strength, since more of the higher-concentration oil is deposited where it will be detected throughout the day rather than dispersed into the surrounding air.</p>
        <p>Peer-reviewed research on aerosol droplet formation and spray dynamics is available through the National Institutes of Health's <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubMed database</a>, which covers atomization physics relevant to consumer aerosol engineering. To understand how orifice geometry and actuator design influence spray coverage across all aerosol categories, read our detailed breakdown of <a href="/aerosol-spray-pattern-engineering-orifice-geometry/" target="_top">aerosol spray pattern engineering</a>.</p>
      </section>

